            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Douglas Engelbart: Early Life</strong><br>\nDouglas Engelbart was born on January 30, 1925, in Portland, Oregon. Growing up during the Great Depression, he was determined to make the world a better place. He served as a radar technician in the Navy during World War II, which sparked his interest in technology. After the war, Engelbart pursued his education, earning a bachelor's degree in electrical engineering and a Ph.D. in engineering.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Invention of the Mouse</strong><br>\nIn the 1960s, Engelbart invented the computer mouse, a device that would revolutionize the way people interact with computers. The first mouse was a simple wooden box with a single button and wheels that turned as it moved. Engelbart envisioned a tool that could navigate a computer's graphical interface, making it more accessible and user-friendly.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Augmenting Human Intellect</strong><br>\nEngelbart's broader vision was to augment human intellect by creating technologies that could help people communicate and solve problems more effectively. He believed that computers could be used not just for computation but also as tools for improving communication and management of information.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Mother of All Demos</strong><br>\nOn December 9, 1968, Engelbart gave a demonstration that became known as \"The Mother of All Demos.\" During this live presentation, he introduced the mouse, video conferencing, teleconferencing, hypertext, word processing, and other revolutionary technologies. This event showcased the potential of computer technology for the future of work and communication.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The oN-Line System (NLS)</strong><br>\nEngelbart developed the oN-Line System (NLS) to implement his ideas. NLS was an early computer system that allowed users to \"click\" on text and \"link\" to other pages. It used a mouse and keyboard interface and was one of the first systems to use hypertext linking, which later inspired the World Wide Web.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Recognition and Awards</strong><br>\nDespite the groundbreaking nature of his work, Engelbart's contributions were not immediately recognized. However, over time, his work gained recognition, and he received numerous awards, including the National Medal of Technology and Innovation in 2000, and the Turing Award, computing's highest honor.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
