          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nEngelbart's later years were spent advocating for the use of technology to solve humanity's pressing issues. He founded the Bootstrap Institute, later known as The Doug Engelbart Institute, which continues to promote his vision of using technology to facilitate collaborative problem-solving.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Challenges and Controversies</strong><br>\nEngelbart faced challenges in getting his ideas accepted. His research funding was cut in the early 1970s, and he struggled to find support for his visionary projects. Moreover, the commercial potential of the mouse was not realized until years later, and Engelbart received no royalties from its widespread adoption.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Conclusion: The Impact of Engelbart's Work</strong><br>\nDouglas Engelbart's work laid the foundation for the interactive computing environments we use today. His invention of the mouse and development of NLS changed the way people interact with computers, making technology more intuitive and accessible to everyone. He passed away on July 2, 2013, leaving a lasting impact on the world of computing.</p>\n"
              },
